  "history": "Same airframe previously wore 'Salmon-Thirty-Salmon II' (whole-body king salmon, 2012) which was retired April 2023 and replaced by this design. The original Salmon-Thirty-Salmon (2005) was on 737-400 N792AS.",
  "notes": "Drawn from the left side (Tampa 2023-11-07, Commons file named for a Delta 757; N559AS taxis in the foreground) and the left from below (JFK 2023-05-29); the right side (2024-07-16) shows the same big shapes. A 2025-03-24 photo shows it still flying in the scheme. The formline is far too intricate for areas: only the big fields are drawn (navy snout, blue cap, blue upper aft fuselage, navy lower aft); the rest is in the logo entries. Commons category for N559AS also holds older Salmon-Thirty-Salmon II photos (2018-2019), not used. Re-checked 2026-11-15 (drawn on the 737-800): the lower aft midnight's edge, a curve rising to the tail cone, made smooth; the rest left as fixed from Alaska's own page. 'Xáat Kwáani' by the cockpit measured on the 2024 right-side photo against the nose and the forward door: 1.7-3.3 m back (x 0.045-0.087). Re-drawn 2026-11-18 at the big masses' scale (docs/LOGOS.md): each side's fields sampled from its own photo (left: JFK 2023-05-29; right: Santa Barbara departure) onto a grid of cells about a third of a metre along by a quarter up, each cell its dominant paint (white, midnight, Atlas), a 3x3 majority pass taking out the linework, the masses' outlines smoothed; the engines, wing and gear in front of the body masked and filled from around them. The fin from the right side's photo (the left's is behind the tailplane), placed by its corners as seen. Pink is only ever thin accents: not an area. The Atlas patch behind the title and the cap round the cockpit are kept as measured (the wing hides the first in the JFK photo). The formline itself stays the logos'.",
  "from": "2023-05",
